, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": -0.14}, "rating_margin": 1.5, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.7, "baseline_margin": 3.94, "analyst_adjustment": -0.94, "key_factors": ["Don Davis's incumbency and strong cash position ($3.6M on hand)", "R+5 district lean offset by a favorable midterm environment for Democrats", "Rater consensus of Tilt D/Tossup with markets pricing Davis near 70%", "Partisan-sponsored polls diverge widely, limiting polling precision"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a narrow Davis edge: a Democratic incumbent in an R+5 district during a midterm environment that favors his party, backed by a large cash advantage and markets pricing him around 70%. Partisan polls on both sides diverge too much to justify moving further, so a modest Democratic margin with wide uncertainty fits the evidence.", "watch": "Republican turnout from Trump's rally and Johnson's visits, plus Buckhout's outside spending, could close the gap if the national environment shifts.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Don Davis (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Don Davis defends a northeastern North Carolina seat that was redrawn to lean Republican, making this one of the state's few genuinely competitive House races. Davis has outraised challenger Laurie Buckhout and holds a large cash advantage, though Republican outside groups are active and national GOP figures have campaigned in the district. Early voting reports suggest strong Democratic turnout so far.\n\nRecent polls are sparse and split along sponsor lines, with Republican-sponsored surveys showing a dead heat and Democratic-sponsored ones showing Davis ahead by high single digits. Most expert raters call the race a Tossup, with a few leaning Democratic, and prediction markets give Davis roughly a two-in-three chance. The race remains genuinely competitive, but the combination of incumbency, money, and the midterm environment gives Davis a modest edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22"